      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2013 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Although postpartum depot-medroxyprogesterone acetate (DMPA) recipients often cite weight gain as the reason for discontinuing DMPA, little is known about body composition changes in postpartum DMPA recipients."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
            "content": "Women who used DMPA during the postpartum year were measured on several anthropometric dimensions of body composition and compared with women who elected surgical sterilization with bilateral partial salpingectomy (BPS)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "After 1 year, DMPA recipients did not differ from the BPS group in weight or percent body fat changes. Almost half the women using DMPA returned to pregravid weight; nearly half gained weight. Higher pre-pregnancy body mass index was associated with weight gain among DMPA recipients."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "DMPA recipients who were overweight or obese before pregnancy may have greater risk for weight gain in the first year postpartum. However, when counseling women, the risk for DMPA-related weight gain should be balanced against the potential for increased weight from subsequent pregnancies."
          }
        ]
      },
